.Dd October 18, 2026
.Dt CARDDAV 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm carddav
.Nd list, show, add and change contacts on a CardDAV server
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl Ajn
.Op Fl B Ns Op Ar dir
.Op Fl a Ar line
.Op Fl b Ar book
.Op Fl d Ar prop
.Op Fl e Ar email
.Op Fl o Ar org
.Op Fl p Ar password
.Op Fl s Ar line
.Op Fl t Ar tel
.Op Fl U Ar url
.Op Fl u Ar user
.Op Fl -debug
.Op Fl -fn Ar name
.Ar command
.Op Ar argument ...
.Nm
.Fl h
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
reads and changes one address book on a CardDAV server.
Options and the command may come in any order.
The commands are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm books
The address books of the home, one line each: id and name.
.It Cm list Op Ar regex
The contacts, by name, one line each: UID, full name and emails.
A
.Ar regex
keeps those whose full name, nickname, email, telephone or organisation
it matches, without regard to case.
.It Cm show Ar contact
The contact's vCard, as the server holds it.
.It Cm add
A new contact made from the options: it needs a full name
.Pq Fl -fn ,
from which its structured name
.Pq Ql N
is made when it has none, the last word as the family name, or what
comes before a comma
.Pq Ql "Sartre, Jean-Paul" .
.It Cm set Ar contact
The contact changed by the options.
.It Cm edit Ar contact
The contact opened in
.Ev EDITOR ,
and stored when the file was changed.
A file that does not parse is offered back to the editor, when there is
a terminal to ask on.
.It Cm import Ar file ...
The vCards of the files, or of stdin for
.Ql - :
a card whose UID the address book has replaces it, any other is
created, and a card with no UID is given one.
When several files hold the same UID, the last one wins, so a
.Fl B
directory restores to its newest copies.
.It Cm delete Ar contact
The contact deleted.
.It Cm backup Op Ar file
Every card of the address book, photos included, saved in one file: to
.Ar file ,
to stdout for
.Ql - ,
or by default to a new
.Pa BOOK-TIMESTAMP.vcf
in the current directory, named after
.Fl b .
With
.Fl A ,
one file per address book,
.Pa ID-TIMESTAMP.vcf ,
in the directory
.Ar file
or the current one.
.Cm import
restores it.
.El
.Pp
A
.Ar contact
is a UID, the name of its resource without
.Pa .vcf ,
or a regular expression matching the full name, a nickname or an email
of exactly one contact; a regex matching several lists them and fails.
.Pp
A contact is stored as vCard 3.0, with a structured name
.Pq Ql N ,
which Cyrus requires, and its
.Ql REV
stamped.
Every change is conditional, so a newer version is never overwritten:
a contact is created only where none is
.Pq Ql If-None-Match: * ,
and replaced or deleted only in the version read
.Pq Ql If-Match .
When it changed on the server meanwhile, the change fails;
.Cm edit
then keeps the edited copy and names it.
.Ss The server and the address book
With
.Fl U ,
the URL is the address book, and its parent the home.
Without it, the home is found from the domain of the user: the
.Ql _carddavs._tcp
SRV record of the domain names the server, its TXT record
.Ql path=
the path, and the home is
.Pa https://server:port/path/user/USER/ ;
.Fl b
then picks an address book in it, by id or by name.
.Ss Credentials
The user is
.Fl u ,
else
.Ev CARDDAV_USER ,
else, with
.Fl U ,
the login of the entry for the URL's host in
.Pa ~/.authinfo ,
then
.Pa ~/.netrc ;
else
.Ev EMAIL .
Without
.Fl U
the user must be one of the options or variables, since the server is
found from it.
The password is
.Fl p ,
else
.Ev CARDDAV_PASSWORD ,
else the password of the server's entry, that of the URL's host or of
the server found, when its login is the user's, else asked on the
terminal.
In those files, lines starting with
.Ql #
are skipped, and a trailing backslash joins a line to the next.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl A , Fl -all
.Cm list
and
.Cm backup :
every address book;
.Cm list
starts each line with the book's name.
.It Fl a Ar line , Fl -add Ns = Ns Ar line
Add a vCard content line, such as
.Ql EMAIL;TYPE=work:jo@example.org ;
repeatable.
.It Fl B Ns Op Ar dir , Fl -backup Ns Op = Ns Ar dir
Save the server's version of each card before changing or deleting it,
as
.Pa UID-TIMESTAMP.vcf
in
.Ar dir ,
by default
.Pa ~/.local/share/carddav .
The directory is attached:
.Fl B Ns Pa dir .
.It Fl b Ar book , Fl -book Ns = Ns Ar book
The address book, by id or name;
.Ql Default
by default.
.It Fl d Ar prop , Fl -delete Ns = Ns Ar prop
Remove the content lines named
.Ar prop ,
in the form
.Ar name Ns Op ; Ns Ar param Ns Op : Ns Ar value :
.Ql EMAIL;TYPE=work
removes the work emails,
.Ql item1.EMAIL
only that group's; repeatable.
One matching nothing is warned about.
.It Fl -debug
Print the HTTP exchange on stderr.
.It Fl e Ar email , Fl -email Ns = Ns Ar email
Add a home email.
.It Fl -fn Ns = Ns Ar name
Set the full name
.Pq Ql FN .
.It Fl h , Fl -help
Print the usage and exit.
.It Fl j , Fl -json
Print JSON: one document for
.Cm books ,
.Cm list
and
.Cm show ,
one object a line for each change.
.It Fl n , Fl -dry-run
Change nothing: print the card that would be stored
.Pq with Fl j , a JSON object holding it ,
or the deletion that would be made.
.It Fl o Ar org , Fl -org Ns = Ns Ar org
Set the organisation.
.It Fl p Ar password , Fl -password Ns = Ns Ar password
The password
.Pq Sx Credentials .
.It Fl s Ar line , Fl -set Ns = Ns Ar line
Replace every content line of that name by this one; repeatable.
.Fl d ,
then
.Fl s ,
then
.Fl a
are applied.
.It Fl t Ar tel , Fl -tel Ns = Ns Ar tel
Add a mobile telephone number.
.It Fl U Ar url , Fl -url Ns = Ns Ar url
The address book, by its URL
.Pq Sx The server and the address book .
.It Fl u Ar user , Fl -user Ns = Ns Ar user
The user
.Pq Sx Credentials .
.El
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width CARDDAV_PASSWORD
.It Ev CARDDAV_PASSWORD
The password, when
.Fl p
is not given.
.It Ev CARDDAV_USER
The user, when
.Fl u
is not given.
.It Ev EDITOR
The editor of
.Cm edit ;
.Xr vi 1
by default.
.It Ev EMAIL
The user, when nothing else names one
.Pq Sx Credentials ;
the address that git and mail tools read too.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa ~/.authinfo , Pa ~/.netrc
The user and password, as the entry of the server
.Pq Ql machine host login user password secret .
.It Pa ~/.local/share/carddav/
Where
.Fl B
saves cards by default.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It 0
Success.
.It 1
A failure: the server or the network failed or refused, no contact or
several matched, the card changed on the server meanwhile, the
directory of
.Cm backup Fl A
is not one, or there is no user or password.
.It 2
A usage error: an unknown option or command, a missing argument, a
regex or URL that does not parse, or
.Cm backup Fl A
to stdout.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Who has an address at example.org:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ carddav list @example.org
.Ed
.Pp
A new contact, then a second email for them:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ carddav add --fn 'Ada Lovelace' -e ada@example.org -t '+44 20 1234'
$ carddav set Lovelace -a 'EMAIL;TYPE=work:ada@work.example'
.Ed
.Pp
Every address book saved, one file each:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ mkdir contacts && carddav -A backup contacts
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr caldav 1
.Sh STANDARDS
.Bl -tag -width "RFC 6764" -compact
.It RFC 2426
vCard MIME Directory Profile; vCard 3.0.
.It RFC 6352
CardDAV: vCard Extensions to Web Distributed Authoring and Versioning
(WebDAV).
.It RFC 6764
Locating Services for Calendaring Extensions to WebDAV (CalDAV) and
vCard Extensions to WebDAV (CardDAV); the SRV and TXT records.
.El
.Sh CAVEATS
.Cm import
stops at the first card the server refuses, the cards before it
stored.
.Pp
.Fl p
puts the password on the command line, where
.Xr ps 1
shows it to every user, and
.Fl -debug
prints it, in the Authorization header; the entry in
.Pa ~/.authinfo
shows it to nobody.
.Pp
.Nm
is Ruby, unlike the other tools of systools: it needs Ruby 3.4 or later
and the nokogiri gem.
